{"name":"Goddess of the Nile","hex":"#5d7f9d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"goddess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female deity.","An incredibly beautiful, gorgeous, sexy, attractive woman honored and adored for her beauty or of superior charm and intelligence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/goddess"}],"entries":[{"title":"Goddess","blocks":[{"text":"A goddess is a female deity. In some faiths, a sacred female figure holds a central place in religious prayer and worship. For example, Shaktism, holds that the ultimate deity, the source of all reality, is Supreme Goddess (Mahamaya) and in some forms of Tantric Shaivism, the pair of Shiva and Shakti are the ultimate principle. Meanwhile, in Vajrayana Buddhism, ultimate reality is often seen as being composed of two principles depicted as two deities in union symbolising the non-duality of the two principles of perfect wisdom (female) and skillful compassion (male). A single figure in a monotheistic faith that is female may be identified simply as god because of no need to differentiate by gender or with a diminutive. An experiment to determine the effect of psychedelics on subjects composed of leaders from diverse religious groups revealed a general experience that the divine which the subjects encountered was feminine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Queen_Nefertari_being_led_by_Isis_MET_DP167142.jpg/330px-Queen_Nefertari_being_led_by_Isis_MET_DP167142.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goddess","wikidata":"Q205985"},{"title":"Nile","blocks":[{"text":"The Nile is a major north-flowing river in northeast Africa which empties into the Mediterranean Sea. At 7,088 kilometers (4,404 mi) long, it is the longest river in the world, although the volume of water it carries is much smaller than other major rivers such as the Amazon or the Congo. The Nile has played a central role in the environmental, economic, and cultural history of Africa for millennia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg/330px-Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile","wikidata":"Q3392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Ceres_statue.jpg/330px-Ceres_statue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Ceres_statue.jpg/330px-Ceres_statue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of an unknown woman as Ceres, Roman goddess of agriculture and motherly relationships","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACeres_statue.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Freia_Gestures_to_Hyndla_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg/500px-Freia_Gestures_to_Hyndla_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Freia_Gestures_to_Hyndla_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg/500px-Freia_Gestures_to_Hyndla_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The goddess Freyja is nuzzled by the boar Hildisvíni while gesturing to Hyndla (1895) by Lorenz Frølich.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFreia_Gestures_to_Hyndla_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Toci_and_Xochiquetzal%2C_Two_Aztec_Goddesses_WDL6731.png/500px-Toci_and_Xochiquetzal%2C_Two_Aztec_Goddesses_WDL6731.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Toci_and_Xochiquetzal%2C_Two_Aztec_Goddesses_WDL6731.png/1280px-Toci_and_Xochiqu